      text: `<p></p> <p>Suppose that we have a set of <em>domino tiles</em>. Each domino piece has a string at the top and a string at the bottom, for example:</p> <p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline abb \\\\ \\hline bc \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \$\$ </p> <p>So a set \\( S \\) of dominos might look like: </p> <p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ S = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline b \\\\ \\hline ca \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline a \\\\ \\hline ab \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline ca \\\\ \\hline a \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline abc \\\\ \\hline c \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\right\\} \$\$ </p> <p>A <em>match</em> for \\( S \\) is an ordered list of one or more dominos from \\( S \\), such that if you read the symbols on the tops, this makes the same string as reading the symbols on the bottom. You can use the same domino more than once in a match, and you do not have to use all the elements of \\( S \\). For example, here is a match for our example set: </p> <p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline a \\\\ \\hline ab \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline b \\\\ \\hline ca \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline ca \\\\ \\hline a \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline a \\\\ \\hline ab \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline abc \\\\ \\hline c \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \$\$ </p> <p>The tops and bottoms of the dominos both form the string <code>abcaaabc</code></p> <p><strong>Consider the following set of tiles:</strong></p> <p style="text-align: center;"><span class="mjx-chtml MJXc-display" style="text-align: center;"></span> \$\$ \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline X \\\\ \\hline U \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline UU \\\\ \\hline U \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline Z \\\\ \\hline X \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline E \\\\ \\hline ZE \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline Y \\\\ \\hline U \\\\ \\hline \\end{array}, \\quad \\begin{array}{|c|} \\hline Z \\\\ \\hline Y \\\\ \\hline \\end{array} \\right\\} \$\$ </p> <p>Let \\( n \\) be the least number of tiles that can be used in a match (counting any repetitions). Let \\( m \\) be the number of different matches using this smallest number of tiles (i.e. \\( n \\)). What is \\( m + n \\) ? </p> <p></p>`,
